# What FT_Read hands back to the vendor software.
#
# Whitespace-separated hex bytes; # starts a comment. The file is re-read whenever it changes, so
# a candidate can be tried by editing this and pressing the button again - no restart, no rebuild.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Attempt 1: leave this empty.
#
# The software pulses DTR and then waits for the cable to announce itself. With nothing to read it
# will give up, exactly as it did in the run-6 USB capture - but this time the log shows the whole
# call sequence and every byte it tried to send, which that capture never revealed.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Attempt 2 onwards: give it an announcement. The strongest clue is the session log shipped with
# the software itself (BMWScan140/2009-2-26_20-02-54/), whose header reads:
#
#     BMW Scanner v1.4.0.6 (24.05.2008) - P.A.Soft, 2008  HW: V144Bn9
#
# "V144Bn9" is a string the host cannot have invented, so the announcement almost certainly carries
# it. Candidates to try, one at a time - uncomment one:
#
# V144Bn9 as plain ASCII:
# 56 31 34 34 42 6E 39
#
# the same, newline-terminated:
# 56 31 34 34 42 6E 39 0D 0A
#
# with a leading length byte, which is how a small microcontroller usually frames a reply:
# 07 56 31 34 34 42 6E 39
